<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1656 CE – 1738 CE · capital Merseburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albertine Saxon secondary duchy on the Saale, created by an elector's will and absorbed back into the Electorate after eighty years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">Elector John George</a> I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> divided parts of his lands in his will among his younger sons. After his death in 1656, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-i-duke-of-saxe-merseburg">Christian I</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a>, the secularised bishopric and its district, founding the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-merseburg">Saxe-Merseburg</a>, which also held parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes held their lands as secundogenitures under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>, with limited independence in foreign affairs. They rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> Castle and maintained a court with music and a library. The duchy was Lutheran and agrarian. Its later dukes were known more for extravagance than for policy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-william-duke-of-saxe-merseburg">Maurice William</a> was nicknamed the Fiddle Duke.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Heinrich, the last duke, died without heirs in 1738, and the territory reverted to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815 and is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1656 CE – 1738 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.66%;width:1.63%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1656 CE – 1738 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Merseburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian I, Maurice William, Heinrich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Merseburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1697&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-merseburg" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 399 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 655L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Merseburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1697&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-merseburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
